{
 "result": "Built wf.py: byte-level word-frequency counter. It reads stdin as bytes, applies a single 256-entry translate table (A-Z -> a-z, every non-[a-z] byte including all non-ASCII -> space), then bytes.split() and a Counter, and finally sorts by (-count, word) to print the top 10 as 'word count'. Because the table maps separators to space and split() collapses runs, tokens are exactly the [a-z]+ runs of the lowercased text, matching the reference semantics (including UTF-8 multibyte sequences being treated as separators, as in the reference split on [^a-z]); ties break by word.\n\nCorrectness is gated by assay.py, which regenerates a 20MB corpus, computes the expected top-10 with an independent reference implementation (decode/lower/re.split('[^a-z]+')/Counter) and compares the program's stdout byte-for-byte; the assay prints 0 on any mismatch, so the recorded nonzero score is itself the correctness proof. The registered assay also times wf.py 5 times and reports bytes/second.\n\nFinal measured fitness: baseline 4.11e+07 -> adopted variant 1.483e+08 bytes/s (~0.14 s wall for the 20MB corpus over 5 runs, i.e. ~148 MB/s), a +244% improvement over the starting implementation, well beyond the 3.7% noise. The winning variant replaced decode+lower+re.findall+Counter with the translate+split+Counter pipeline; earlier micro-benchmarks confirmed translate ~9ms vs split ~73ms vs counting ~204ms, so counting (not tokenizing) now dominates, and fork/pickle sharding and numpy approaches were tested and found slower or unreliable.",
 "calls": 16,
 "secs": 646.1
}
